"""The Block Camp hub in the ten languages the decks offer: the data table
build.py reads. Nothing here is imported by the quest, nav, flags or village
builders - they load build.py for its CLIMB / DESCENT / ADVENTURES tables, and
build.py only reads this file inside build().

Innes, 2026-10-03: "block camp hub needs a translation option and button".

HOW THE PAGE USES THIS. English is the page source (search, no-script
visitors, check-access.py). Every translated element carries
data-i18n="key" (its innerHTML), data-i18n-aria="key" (aria-label) or
data-i18n-title="key" (title); the page keeps the English it found and swaps
in UI[lang][key]. A key whose element has data-n="9" fills {n} from it.
Counts ({tenses}, {adv}, {climb}, {descent}, {refs}, {total}) and the free
clause ({free}) are filled in by build.py, so the page carries finished
strings. {k|one|few|many} spells the noun after a count by the language's
plural rule (Russian: one/few/many; Arabic: one/two/few/many/other).

The choice is the line's, not the page's: localStorage 'bc-lang' (beside
'bc-cc', 'bc-music' and 'bc-music-vol'), and ?lang=xx sets it.

WHAT IS NOT TRANSLATED, on purpose, as in the decks: tense and camp names
(Present Simple, Going To Passive, The Trial - every deck keeps its cover
title English in every language, and camp-nav.js says so: "Camp names stay
English, as they do everywhere in the line"), the grammar chips on the
adventures, lesson names in "More Minecraft Lessons", levels, "Pro", the
BLOCK CAMP wordmark, Grammar Stoker's Blocula (a pun on a name), and the
page's title and meta, which seo.py owns.

The words follow the decks' own where they exist: camp / station / part /
Lookout / "Next:" from camp-end.js, route map / descent / adventures from
camp-nav.js, "Go Pro" from camp-end.js (shortened for the top band in
Russian and Portuguese), the adventure titles from each RPG's own cover
where it translates one. Portuguese is Brazilian, as camp-nav, camp-full and
camp-end are.
"""

